1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The tenth process will be the finishing,

2
00:00:05,024 --> 00:00:08,024
or as coined on the East Coast as

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,500
<i>menyelek</i>. This process requires the use of shellac.

4
00:00:12,524 --> 00:00:17,524
People from yesteryear will use shellac
made from rubber wood

5
00:00:17,548 --> 00:00:21,548
soaked overnight in a solution of spirit

6
00:00:21,572 --> 00:00:24,572
and the top part will be skimmed
and used as shellac.

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,700
A brush will be used to apply shellac on the surface

8
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:34,724
and base of the hilt, spreading it thinly and evenly

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
throughout the entire surface including its nooks

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
and crannies and even bored holes.

11
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:55,400
The first layer will be applied thinly

12
00:00:55,424 --> 00:00:59,424
and will be hung to dry

13
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:11,448
for a period of 2 to 3 hours to warrant a truly dry shellac.

14
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:17,200
The purpose of this finishing or <i>menyelek</i>
is to enhance the outlook

15
00:01:17,224 --> 00:01:24,224
of the keris hilt giving it a more radiant and smoother finish.

16
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:33,800
Properly maintained the layer of shellac

17
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:39,824
can protect for up to 10 - 20 years,

18
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and if the shellac is of pristine quality
it could even last indefinitely.
